@meta
id: 09-08
title: A count on each side multiplies
chapter: 09
type: lesson
skills: op-counts
par: 22
minutes: 5
vscode: two identical drag-selects and two Deletes
open: policy.md

@brief
Two near-identical lines, both too wordy:

    Refunds are sent back to the original payment card within ten working days.
    Chargebacks are sent back to the original payment card within ten working days.

Both should read `... are sent within ten working days.` — six words come
out of each line.

Do the first line with a count on the motion. Do the second with the count
on the operator instead. Convince yourself they land in the same place.

@keys
| `d6w` | delete six words — count on the motion |
| `2d3w` | delete twice three words — counts on both |
| `j0` | down a line and back to column one |

@hints
You already know the first form. For the second, the number goes before
the `d`.
--
A count in front of the operator and a count in front of the motion are
multiplied together. `2d3w` is `d6w`.
--
`3w d6w`, then `j0 3w 2d3w`.

@solution
    3w d6w    j 0 3w 2d3w    :wq<Enter>

Both lines came out identical, which is the whole lesson: `2d3w` and
`d6w` are the same edit. Vim multiplies the two counts and applies the
result to the motion. `3d2j` is `d6j`. `2y4w` is `y8w`.

Nobody types `2d3w` on purpose. The reason to know the rule is that it
explains why `2dd` deletes two lines rather than doing something exotic,
and it stops you from inventing a meaning for the first number that does
not exist. There is only ever one number, and it belongs to the motion.

The second reason is that the count in front of the operator is the one
your hand reaches for when you are already thinking in repetitions — "do
that three times" — and it is worth knowing that vim will quietly turn
that into a single larger motion rather than repeating the edit three
times. For `d` the distinction does not matter. When you get to the dot
command and to macros in later chapters, it will.

@start policy.md
Refunds are sent back to the original payment card within ten working days.
Chargebacks are sent back to the original payment card within ten working days.

@expected policy.md
Refunds are sent within ten working days.
Chargebacks are sent within ten working days.
